=== THE MOON: "most of one turn lost in nearly thirty thousand" ===
turns of the main wheel by the stars: 2230.49; the Moon's pointer: 254/19 x that = 29,818.1 turns (with 2,230.5: 29,818.3)
lost by the slow running alone: 262.2 deg = 0.728 of a turn; read from where the pointer stands on the day (360 - 105.3): 0.707 of a turn
one turn lost in 40,947; "most of one turn in nearly thirty thousand" = 0.73 in 29,818

=== "In short": the Sun, Venus and Saturn (run.out) ===
Sun on the day, against the stars: +0.12 deg (rms over the 76 years 0.18, from run.out); with the keeper by the seasons, against the seasons: +1.14 (rms 0.88)
typical error then -> now (run.out, frame S): Venus 1.48 -> 10.44 (x7.0); Saturn 4.97 -> 9.26 (x1.86); Mercury x1.11, Mars x1.22, Jupiter x1.21
typical error today: Mercury 4.7, Venus 10.4, Mars 11.7, Jupiter 4.4, Saturn 9.3 (so Venus and Saturn have "got worse" than they were, and are not worse than Mars is)

catalogue: 5776 lunar and 5674 solar eclipses read (the dial note: 5,776 and 5,674)

=== THE ECLIPSE DIAL by each published list, kind for kind (my recount from the catalogue pages) ===
Freeth: my counts agree with two_lists.json in all 123 cycles: True
   months marked for the Moon: none with a lunar eclipse from cycle 64 (and never again: True); 64 x 18.03 = 1153.9 years on -> AD 950
   months marked for the Sun:  none with a solar eclipse from cycle 70 (and never again: True); 70 x 18.03 = 1262.1 years on -> AD 1058
   the last lunar eclipse in a month marked for the Moon: 13 Jun AD 949 (cell 214, cycle 63); the last solar eclipse in a month marked for the Sun: 30 May AD 1052 (cell 149, cycle 69)
   (to the end of the catalogue pages, AD 2100, nothing later: True)
   so no month has held the eclipse it is marked for in the 974 years since 1052; counted from the page's date for the cycle, 968 years
   months marked only for the Moon (23) holding eclipses of the SUN: the last 10 Jun AD 1526 (cell 214); 233 of them from cycle 70 on
   months marked only for the Sun holding eclipses of the MOON: from the dial's first month on (cycles 0 to 122 and the one now running): 0; in the catalogue's years before the dial's first month (300 to 205 BC, not the page's span): 8
Iversen and Jones: my counts agree with two_lists.json in all 123 cycles: True
   months marked for the Moon: none with a lunar eclipse from cycle 62 (and never again: True); 62 x 18.03 = 1117.9 years on -> AD 914
   months marked for the Sun:  none with a solar eclipse from cycle 73 (and never again: True); 73 x 18.03 = 1316.2 years on -> AD 1112
   the last lunar eclipse in a month marked for the Moon: 23 Jun AD 902 (cell 79, cycle 61); the last solar eclipse in a month marked for the Sun: 20 Apr AD 1110 (cell 196, cycle 72)
   (to the end of the catalogue pages, AD 2100, nothing later: True)
   so no month has held the eclipse it is marked for in the 916 years since 1110; counted from the page's date for the cycle, 914 years
   months marked only for the Moon (21) holding eclipses of the SUN: the last 11 Jul AD 1515 (cell 79); 159 of them from cycle 73 on
   months marked only for the Sun holding eclipses of the MOON: from the dial's first month on (cycles 0 to 122 and the one now running): 0; in the catalogue's years before the dial's first month (300 to 205 BC, not the page's span): 3

